purpose:
Serves both purposes.

Purpose 1 (co-evolution). The verification side needs:
- a rule for selecting and weighting the items its gate uses;
- a validity test for generated items, so that what it feeds back to the Skill side is measurable;
- a fitness for the verifier's own evolving artifact. Here that artifact is a set of generator families, scored by family-level transfer-predictiveness TPS_F, slope and bias against real training items.

Purpose 2 (generalisation metric: evolve on one benchmark, evaluate on the others). The evaluation needs:
- per-benchmark panels whose numbers generalise beyond their own items (the population estimand);
- known item counts per effect size (D-study);
- stratified weighting per benchmark.

what_i_did:
The work ran in two sessions of the same exploration.

Earlier session (2026-10-20 19:20 to 2026-10-21 01:08)
- Extracted the data, all training-side only:
  - all cal-1 cells (8,800 main = attempt-1 7,189 + resume 1,611, plus the 400-cell pre-round), read-only on the old machine;
  - the v3 non-evaluation cells (train/screen/confirm; SearchQA 6,819 on the 200 focal items, plus the other four benchmarks);
  - the 10/15-16 solver-strength probe cells (15,096; labels skill_confirmation/train/val only).
- Fitted item statistics:
  - difficulty;
  - G-theory variance components;
  - per-text split-half reliability of item deltas against a parametric null;
  - Storey pi0 "items changed";
  - cross-validated concentration;
  - net effect by parent stratum;
  - Rasch/2PL with Skill texts as respondents, deviance split and residual SVD against parallel analysis.
- Tested no-test-data item properties as predictors (AUC) of responders and harm-susceptible items.
- Dead-item analysis over independent sessions.
- Retrospective panel simulation: random / stratified / Neyman / drop-dead / most-movable / anchor-points / p-IRT, with shared and independent runs.
- D-study: items vs runs, population vs fixed-panel estimand, on all five benchmarks.
- Generated-vs-real validity from the probe data.
- Simulation calibrating a family-level transfer-predictiveness score TPS_F.
- Literature survey.
- Pre-registered pilot E5-P1: analysis plan and analysis code sha256-hashed before launch. Phase A and the first half of phase B ran before the session stopped.

Resumed session (2026-10-21 04:41-05:20)
- Re-computed the headline numbers with my own independent code; exact match:
  - arm counts;
  - strata 37/52/111;
  - by-stratum nets for ids-full / control:3 / cand / seed-rule3;
  - variance shares 63.8/1.3/11.6/23.2 %.
- Checked the D-study arithmetic by hand.
- Re-checked 10 arXiv sources (abstracts; the bodies of PromptEval and Miller).
- Corrected 5 draft claims:
  - tinyBenchmarks' abstract has no error figure;
  - Signal-and-Noise shows no venue and its "noise" is checkpoint variability;
  - item-text features are weak rather than useless;
  - D8's dead-item count is 25, not 26;
  - the D-study range is 220-370, not 250-370.
- Completed the pilot's missing 720 cells: dry runs first, then 6 workers on each machine, same frozen runner and strata, identity checks 16/16.
- Merged the cells from both machines into a new directory.
- Ran the pre-registered analysis (hashes verified), plus an exploratory split by serving window (labelled as not pre-registered).
- Wrote the final report.

Nothing was written to the repository or to any completed run directory, and no test, holdout or evaluation path was opened.
